{
  "library": "basic",
  "description": "General-purpose outline shapes and annotation marks, the set draw.io, Lucidchart and Visio put in their basic palettes: ellipse, triangle, trapezoid, chevron, cross, arrow, double-arrow, note, callout, brace, cube, folder and component. Each is placed as the presentation shape custom:basic/<name>, a branch the document schema already admits, so a shape needs no kind of its own: put it on any kind (process in a flowchart, service in an architecture diagram, note for an annotation) and the shape only changes how the box is drawn. The UML marks are here too: a use case is an ellipse, a package is a folder, a deployment node is a cube, and a component is a component. A shape is data, not code: every outline is path data in the node's box, so the web canvas (web/src/model/basicShapes.ts) and the MCP SVG renderer (src/Draughtsman.Core/Schemas/BasicShapeLibrary.cs, drawn by src/Draughtsman.Mcp/Rendering/SvgRenderer.cs) read the same numbers and cannot drift. Path syntax: the absolute commands M, L, C, H, V and Z only. Each coordinate is a fraction of the box (x of the width, y of the height) written as a decimal, or a fraction plus or minus a fixed length in pixels such as \"1-16\" (16 px in from the far edge) or \"0+8\" (8 px in from the near edge); a fixed length is never more than half the box, so a small box keeps its shape. The first part is the outline, painted with the node's fill and stroke; every later part is a detail painted with the node's stroke, and with its fill too when it says fill true. A part with fill false (the brace) draws a line only. labelBox is the label's rectangle as [left, top, right, bottom] in the same coordinates. ports are where a connector lands, as plain fractions of the box (the four side midpoints unless the outline does not pass through them); a shape lists only the ports it has. size is the box the palette places the shape at. keywords are the other names people search the palette by. annotation true marks a shape that comments on the diagram rather than takes part in it (the palette places it on the note kind).",
